+6+2+4=90.\n\nAlternatively, AddRoundKey option 2 + SubBytes option 1 + MixColumns option 1 → sum 3682.9 (3783.8), latency 87.\n\nThe latter has a slightly higher area but lower latency. Since the goal is to minimize latency, this is better, even if the area is slightly further from the target.\n\nBut how much is the difference in area? 3783.8 vs 3779.8 → 4 units. The target is 3800, so the former is 16.2 under, the latter 20.2 under. The former is closer. \n\nSo between the two, the combination with area 3783.8 (16.2 under) and latency 87 is better than area 3779.8 (20.2 under) and latency 90. \n\nAnother angle: perhaps the total area can be slightly over the target if it allows significantly lower latency. For example, if a combination has area 3800 + x (x positive), but x is small, and latency is much lower. But from the given options, it\'s hard to find such a combination because the areas of the individual functions don\'t allow it. \n\nFor instance, if we take AddRoundKey option 2 (1620.1), SubBytes option 1 (1160.4), MixColumns option 1 (902.4) → sum 3682.9 → total 3783.8. To reach 3800, we need an additional 16.2. But there\'s no way to add that without changing the options. \n\nAlternatively, if we use a higher area option for SubBytes. For example, SubBytes option 3 (1160.4) is the same as option 1. No help. Or SubBytes option 2 (1889.1) which increases area but also latency. \n\nIf we use SubBytes option 2 (1889.1) with AddRoundKey option 1 (752.6) and MixColumns option 1 (902.4):\n\nSum area:752.6 +1889.1 +902.4=3543.1 → total 3644.0. Latency:3*34=102 +15 +2 +4=123. Worse latency.\n\nSo no improvement.\n\nGiven all this, the best combination seems to be:\n\nAddRoundKey_hls option 2 (area 1620.1, latency 26)\n\nSubBytes_hls option 1 (area 1160.4, latency 3)\n\nMixColumns_hls option 1 (area 902.4, latency 2)\n\nShiftRows_hls and xtime options don\'t matter since their areas and latencies are fixed.\n\nThis gives total area 3783.8 and latency 87. \n\nNow, the user\'s options include Cipher_hls, but according to the problem statement, the Cipher_hls options have their own area and latency. However, if the total area is determined by the sum of the selected function options, then including Cipher_hls would add its area to the total. But according to the initial analysis, the Cipher_hls\'s area already includes the child functions. This is conflicting. \n\nWait, the problem statement says that the options for each function include their child functions when synthesized in isolation. Therefore, if we select an option for Cipher_hls, its area and latency already include the child functions\' areas and latencies. Therefore, the total area would be the area of Cipher_hls\'s option plus any other HLS functions not included in it. But according to the call graph, main calls AES_init_ctx, Cipher, and phex. AES_init_ctx calls KeyExpansion, which has no HLS options. Cipher is implemented as Cipher_hls. Therefore, the total area would be the area of Cipher_hls\'s option. But the Cipher_hls options have areas ranging from ~5800 to 6500, which are all above 3800. This suggests that the previous approach is incorrect, and the Cipher_hls\'s area is the total area, and the target of 3800 is unattainable. But that\'s impossible, so there must be a misunderstanding. \n\nThis indicates that perhaps the initial assumption about how areas are aggregated is wrong. Maybe the total area is the sum of the areas of all the HLS functions selected, but Cipher_hls\'s options do not include the child functions. For example, Cipher_hls\'s area is its own logic, and the child functions\' areas are added separately. \n\nBut according to the problem statement, when a function is synthesized in isolation, it includes the child functions. Therefore, the area reported for Cipher_hls\'s options includes the areas of its child functions. So if we select Cipher_hls option 0 (area 6500.2), that area already includes AddRoundKey_hls, SubBytes_hls, etc. So the t